<h1>adaptive Huffman coding</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A near-minimal variable-length character coding that changes based on the frequency of characters processed.  As characters are processed, frequencies are updated and codes are changed (or, the coding tree is modified).
</p>

<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> dynamic Huffman coding.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="huffmanCoding.html"><em>Huffman coding</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="vittersAlgorithm.html"><em>Vitter's algorithm</em></a>, <a href="algorithmFGK.html"><em>algorithm FGK</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The total message length can be less than that produced by a static Huffman coding since the coding can be different at different places in the message.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<a href="http://www.ics.uci.edu/~dan/pubs/DC-Sec4.html" target="_blank">Explanation</a> of algorithm FGK and Vitter's Algorithm (algorithm V), two different adaptive Huffman coding algorithms.
</p>

